A||||9||Songman||Indy brakes||||5||
Z||000000||Songman||05-16-2001||10:39 PM||||OK, here's the story.. I'm taking my girlfriend to the mall today and we keep smelling brakes. Plus, I noticed that when I pushed the clutch in at lights that the car didn't roll even when I didn't have the brake in... and I had a helluva pull to one side as well...<P>Pulled over, got out and found a front brake smoking. Figured I'd skip the mall and head home which was about 15 miles away. The smell and the pull got real bad so I got off on a city street so I could go slower. Then, it really got bad. The pedal dropped to the floor. I pulled over expecting to see brake fluid all over the wheel, but I didn't. Figured I could get home using the gears as brakes. All the idiots that pulled out in front of me are for another post...<P>Here's the question... I want to keep the Indy stock, BUT, do I just go replace the brakes with stock parts or would it hurt to do one of the upgrades that everyone talks about? Any thoughts?<P>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||05-16-2001||11:08 PM||||Up grades.  That is up to you. I have done it to my wifes GT and had great results. But it is more expensive than it seams.  I think too if I had of put the money into QUALITY brake pads, I could have corrected my problems for alot less $$.  Sounds like a frt caliper has seized or a brake flex line has collapsed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||05-17-2001||07:16 AM||||I wouldn't unless your going to be doing some road racing..||||reg||3
Z||000003||Wolfhound||05-17-2001||07:56 AM||||Songman, See if the brakes work after cooling. If not the caliper is damaged from heat. If so the caliper survived and you just boiled the fluid. Jack up the side of the car that over heated. Apply the brakes. check to see if the front wheel is locked. If so loosen the bleeder valve with no pedal pressure applied and see if the caliper releases. If it does it's the rubber brake line. Replace front pads and rubber line. <BR> If the caliper is damaged, I'll let you have working caliper off a parts car for shipping.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-17-2001||10:41 AM||||Having Fiero calipers freeze is fairly common if the car has been sitting for a long time. Heard of several cars doing this.<BR>Before you do an upgrade, please go to Ogre's cave and read the articles on brakes and brake upgrades. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-17-2001||10:44 AM||||It sounds like the pedal going to the floor is a result of over heating. Once you determine what is bad, (caliper or line) I would suggest a total brake line flush with new fluid. Also If the line went bad, You should think about replacing them all. Usually when one goes bad the others follow.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <BR>Got Emblems?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||05-17-2001||05:46 PM||||Thanks guys...<P>The brakes did work again after cooling. The only thing that I think of now is that I hadn't been driving the car that long and I was on open interstate. Is it still possible that the fluid overheated?<P>I think for step one I will replace pads, lines, and fluid with stock parts. Also, the car has sat for a long time so that could play a part. I'll turn the rotors and everything.<P>Coy, I appreciate the offer for the caliper. May take you up on it at a later date if that is okay..<P>Thanks a lot guys... off to Ogre's cave to read more..<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wolfhound||05-17-2001||06:41 PM||||Songman, No problem. <BR>After you replace the front lines and pads just keep an eye on the caliper for leaks. The heat may or may not have damaged the seals inside. They take a fair amount of heat at normal use.<BR>Coy<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||ironhedno3||05-17-2001||08:25 PM||||i aggree with all the above..caliper was seized from setting too long..yes you did boil the fluid and yes there may be a leak as a result..let me know when ya get ready to upgrade..its pretty easy||||reg||6
Z||000009||beachbomb||05-17-2001||10:25 PM||||Hey songman, i had the exact same thing happen on my indy, the hydraulic cable collapsed, i went ahead and overhauled the whole darn brake system while i was at it..lmk how it comes out..Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||1
